  "Source": "X. Li, X. Yu, and Y. Han, \"Polymer thin films for antireflection coatings,\" J. Mater. Chem. C 1, 2266-2285 (2013). doi:10.1039/c2tc00529h - Fig. 3 caption p. 2269 ('a 99 nm thick MgF2 coating (triangles)'), MgF2 n = 1.38 from text p. 2268; from ref. 26 (Walheim et al., Science 283, 520 (1999))",
  "Notes": "Conventional single-layer MgF2 antireflection coating on a glass slide, the comparison curve in Fig. 3c\nSpecification: Single quarter-wave MgF2 AR layer (comparison only).\nReported: No numbers: only the transmittance curve in Fig. 3c (triangles), compared with the nanoporous PMMA film (99.95 % at 534 nm, p. 2269) and an uncoated glass slide (squares). Text p. 2268: MgF2 (n = 1.38) is too high in index to reach zero reflectance on glass (n_ideal = 1.23). Expected single-surface minimum R = ((1.52 - 1.38^2)/(1.52 + 1.38^2))^2 ~ 1.26 % at 4 n d = 546 nm (computed, not printed).\nNotes: Whether the slide was coated on one or both sides, and the slide index, are not stated (glass n = 1.52 assumed, semi-infinite). The figure plots transmittance of a slide (two surfaces), so the curve cannot be compared quantitatively with a front-surface-only computation; listed mainly because the layer is fully specified. reference_nm = 4 n d, not stated in the paper.\nLayers from Fig. 3 caption p. 2269 ('a 99 nm thick MgF2 coating (triangles)'), MgF2 n = 1.38 from text p. 2268; from ref. 26 (Walheim et al., Science 283, 520 (1999)); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nNot checked: the paper reports this design only as plots.",
